{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794137235,"nonce":"gYfRDpaw9Qloux9L3kOsNTgyLhs=","title":"Narrow render backend interface: define what Blinc needs from a GPU and put wgpu behind it","message":"First step of the lean GPU plan. Today blinc_gpu and blinc_app call wgpu directly: 208 distinct wgpu items, about 3,300 mentions in blinc_gpu and 588 in blinc_app, 39 render pipelines and 5 compute pipelines. Bind-group plumbing (BindGroupEntry, BindGroupLayoutEntry, BindingType, BindingResource) is roughly 480 of those mentions, the biggest single block.\n\nDefine the smallest interface the renderer needs: buffers and textures with their upload and copy paths, samplers, the pipelines and passes it builds, surface acquire and present, and capability queries (storage buffers, independent blend, BC formats, MSAA counts), since the WebGL2 and low-end paths already branch on them. Implement it on wgpu first, with no behaviour change, so every golden still passes.\n\nDesign question to settle here, because it shapes every later backend: keep wgpu-style bind groups, or move to a root-arguments model (one pointer or small table per draw, bindless indices) as in sebbbi/NoGraphicsAPI. That model maps natively to Metal 4 and Vulkan 1.4 descriptor heaps but needs emulation on WebGL2, GLES and older Vulkan, which lack buffer device addresses and storage buffers.\n\nParent: the lean GPU stack plan. Related: 346dacb.","files":null}]}